---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CombineImages from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CombineImages.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="combine-images">
  <CombineImages client:load defaultLayout="horizontal" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Three ways to combine images</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Side by side: images go from left to right and are matched on height. Best for before-and-after pairs, comparisons and wide banners.</li>
      <li>Stacked: top to bottom, matched on width. Best for joining screenshots of a long page, a chat thread or a receipt scanned in two halves.</li>
      <li>Grid: a fixed number of columns, each cell sized to the largest image. Best for contact sheets, collages and photo sets.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the sizing options work</h2>
    <p>
      Photos are seldom the same size, and how you handle that decides if the montage looks tidy. The
      tool gives you three explicit choices:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Setting</th><th>What happens</th><th>Use when</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Keep every image at its own size</td><td>Nothing is scaled; shorter images are padded with the background colour and positioned by the alignment control</td><td>The images are already consistent, or you want the size difference to show</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scale all to the smallest</td><td>All images are reduced to the height (or width) of the smallest image</td><td>The default for comparisons: no image is enlarged, so nothing goes soft</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scale all to the largest</td><td>All images are enlarged to match the biggest</td><td>You need a specific output size and accept upscaling of the small ones</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Scaling always keeps the aspect ratio of each image and uses a high-quality resample, so a
      4000 px photo reduced to match a 1200 px one still looks clean.
    </p>

    <h2>Gaps, colour and corners</h2>
    <p>
      A gap of 8–24 px in a contrasting colour stops a montage from looking like one confusing wide
      photograph. Set the background to white for print and documents, or to your page colour for the
      web. If the montage will be on an unknown background, turn on transparency and export PNG.
      Corner rounding clips each photo to a rounded rectangle: 8–16 px looks modern, and above 40 px it
      looks like a sticker sheet.
    </p>

    <h2>Order matters, and you control it</h2>
    <p>
      You can drag the file chips above the preview. The order is left to right for side by side, top
      to bottom for stacked, and row by row (left to right, then down) for the grid. Remove an image
      with the × on its chip; the canvas updates immediately. The pixel dimensions of the output show
      above the preview, so you know what you will download.
    </p>

    <h2>Why there is no file limit</h2>
    <p>
      Upload services limit the number of images, the size and the jobs per day, because each job
      costs them bandwidth and CPU. Here the compositing is a canvas operation in your browser, so the
      only limit is the memory of your device. That limit is real: a grid of twenty 12-megapixel photos
      makes a canvas of several hundred million pixels, and some mobile browsers refuse to allocate it.
      In that case, make the inputs smaller with <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a> and try
      again.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing the export format</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>PNG: lossless, keeps transparency, the correct default for screenshots, diagrams and images with text.</li>
      <li>JPG: much smaller for photographic montages; no transparency, so the background colour is always painted.</li>
      <li>WebP: smaller than JPG at the same quality and keeps transparency; the best option for the web when you know the browsers of your audience.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If the combined file must fit a size limit, use <a href="/compress-image-to-1mb">compress to 1 MB</a>
      next. It shows the quality setting it chose.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/merge-images-side-by-side">Merge Images Side by Side</a> opens in the horizontal preset
      for comparison shots, and <a href="/photo-collage-maker">Photo Collage Maker</a> opens on a
      2-column grid with gaps and rounded corners already set. To caption the finished montage, use
      <a href="/add-text-to-image">Add Text to Image</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
